IEEE 802.11 standards: A suite of Wi-Fi technology standards established by the institute of Electric and Electronic Engineers (IEEE),
such as 802.11b, 802.11g, 802.11n, 802.11ac, and 802.11ax, which define the rules for wireless communications in local area networks.

Wi-Fi is a wireless technology employed for the local area networking of devices.
Its development drew from similar protocols and technology as Ethernet, encompassing IP at the network layer and TCP and UDP at the 
transport layer. Common technologies include routers, switches, the domain name system (DNS), and, in most cases, wireless networks connect
to wired networks.

